---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "The Guild House",
  servesCuisine: ["American", "Contemporary American"],
  priceRange: "$$",
  telephone: "+1-614-280-9780",
  url: "https://theguildhousecolumbus.com/",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "624 N High St",
    addressLocality: "Columbus",
    addressRegion: "OH",
    postalCode: "43215",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Cameron Mitchell artisan eatery in Columbus's Short North Arts District, next to Le Méridien Columbus The Joseph, serving creative New American cuisine from breakfast through dinner.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Guild House Menu Guide (2026) — Prices & What to Order"
  description="An independent guide to The Guild House in Columbus's Short North: signature dishes like Tuna Ribbons and Pork Cheeks, reported price bands, happy hour, and visiting tips. Prices reported as of October 2026."
  path="/guild-house-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/guild-house-dining.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/guild-house-dining.webp"
      alt="Illustration of The Guild House's artisan dining room in Columbus's Short North"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · Short North, Columbus, Ohio
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      The Guild House — Menu Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-soft">By <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Firoz Khan</span> <span class="text-gold-dark">&middot;</span> FK Digital Media</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      The Guild House sits at 624 North High Street in the Short North Arts District, next
      door to the Le Méridien Columbus The Joseph hotel — one important piece of honesty
      first: it's not inside the hotel, but it serves as its de facto dining room, and it's
      a Cameron Mitchell restaurant, which in Columbus carries real weight. The concept is a
      modern artisan eatery: creative New American food served from breakfast through dinner,
      with repurposed wood, local art, and a strong cocktail program. Below is our independent
      walkthrough of the menu, reported price bands, and what we'd order. Everything is
      written in our own words; prices are reported figures, not a copy of the restaurant's
      menu.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">624 N High St, Columbus, OH 43215 (Short North, adjacent to Le Méridien Columbus The Joseph)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Chef</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Sonny Pache (Cameron Mitchell Restaurants)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Cuisine</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">New American, artisan / farm-to-table</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Price level</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">$31–$50 per person (OpenTable)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Reservations</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Recommended for dinner and weekend brunch; book via OpenTable</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (614) 280-9780</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>Signature dishes</h2>
      <p>
        The menu turns over with the seasons, but a core set of signatures has defined the
        restaurant since it opened: <strong>Tuna Ribbons</strong> (ahi tuna, avocado, radish,
        chili oil, yuzu sesame dressing), <strong>Pork Cheeks</strong> (tomato gravy, spaghetti
        squash, charred shishito peppers), <strong>Prime Rib Cap</strong> (sweet onion relish,
        Pointe Reyes blue cheese, mustard vinaigrette), <strong>Scallops</strong> (English peas,
        baby carrots, parsnip puree, orange vinaigrette), and <strong>Seasonal Doughnuts</strong>.
        These are the dishes reviewers write home about, and the pork cheeks and prime rib cap
        are the two to prioritize if it's your first visit.
      </p>

      <h2>Price bands</h2>
      <p>
        Published reviews from earlier years put appetizers around <strong>$12–$17</strong> and
        entrées around <strong>$21–$34</strong> — think pork cheeks at $15 as a starter, a chicken
        roulade around $27, and a ribeye near $34. Those figures are dated (from 2015–2020
        reviews), so treat them as the shape of the menu rather than today's numbers; current
        prices should be confirmed with the restaurant. Diner favorites that recur in recent
        reviews include lamb chops with fried rice, the smoked chicken hash at brunch, and a
        chocolate peanut butter torte for dessert.
      </p>

      <h2>Hours &amp; happy hour</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> daily, 7am–11am</li>
        <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Monday–Friday, 11am–3pm</li>
        <li><strong>Brunch:</strong> Saturday–Sunday, 11am–3pm</li>
        <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Monday–Saturday, 3pm–10pm; Sunday, 3pm–9pm</li>
        <li><strong>Happy hour:</strong> Monday–Friday, 4pm–6pm</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The happy hour is the value play — reviewers consistently call it one of the better
        deals in the Short North.
      </p>

      <h2>What we'd order</h2>
      <p>If we had one meal here, this is how we'd play it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>To start:</strong> the Tuna Ribbons — light, precise, and the signature opener.</li>
        <li><strong>The main event:</strong> the Pork Cheeks. Fork-tender, unctuous, and the dish the kitchen is known for.</li>
        <li><strong>Or the splurge:</strong> the Prime Rib Cap — an uncommon cut that beef lovers rave about.</li>
        <li><strong>To finish:</strong> the Seasonal Doughnuts, or the chocolate peanut butter torte if it's on.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>It's a Cameron Mitchell house</strong> — service is polished and the room handles business dinners, dates, and celebrations with equal ease.</li>
        <li><strong>Book weekend brunch ahead.</strong> Locals call it their number-one brunch spot, and it fills up.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget:</strong> at $31–$50 per person, it's upscale without being precious — dinner for two with drinks lands in the low-to-mid hundreds.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Individual dish prices above come from <strong>published reviews from 2015–2020</strong>
        and are likely dated — we present them as the menu's shape, not current figures. Always
        confirm prices with the restaurant before visiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/best-hotel-restaurants-ohio/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to Ohio hotel restaurants</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
